# Moderation

- Moderation never writes another plugin's data. An action on a reported target is a form in the Reports panel that posts to the owning plugin's own route (`annotations/<id>/hide/`, `submissions/close/`). That route checks `Origin`, its own permission with `can()`, writes its own audit entry, and redirects to a validated same-site `returnTo`. Moderation shows the form only when the viewer holds that permission, and only when the plugin is on the vault (`ctx.plugins` at build time); `can()` throws for a permission no plugin declared.
- Reading another plugin's data goes through an export of its server entry (`findComment` from `@svartz/plugin-annotations/server`), with the binding name moderation's build read from that plugin's options. Never query another plugin's tables directly.
- Resolving a report and acting on its target stay separate actions, each audited.
- Every reportable thing is a button marked `data-svartz-report="<kind>:<id>"` with `data-svartz-report-label`; moderation's browser script answers it by delegation. A plugin adds Report to its own views this way, without importing moderation.
- The panel never renders who reported. Every reader-supplied string (reason, comment body, names) goes through `escape`.
- Commits and submissions are validated by form only (SHA, number) because moderation doesn't call GitHub; the owning route checks the rest when a moderator acts. Revisit once the submissions mirror lands.
